A Golod group is a finitely generated infinite residually finite p-group (for some prime p) every finitely generated subgroup of which is finite, constructed via the Golod–Shafarevich inequality applied to a graded associative algebra with few relations; equivalently, an infinite finitely generated p-group obtained as a subgroup of the adjoint group 1+J of a nil algebra J of Golod–Shafarevich type, so that every finitely generated subgroup is a finite p-group while the whole group is infinite.

An AT-group (in the sense of Rozhkov) is an infinite group G acting faithfully by automorphisms on a spherically homogeneous rooted tree T such that:
(i) G acts level-transitively on T;
(ii) every element of G has finite order (G is a periodic group);
(iii) the stabilizers in G of vertices of T satisfy a prescribed self-similar/branch condition making G a so-called "AT" (Aleshin-type / "almost-tree") group, namely G is generated by finitely many automorphisms of T whose action on each subtree below the first level is again, up to a permutation of subtrees, an element of (a fixed system of) generators of G.

Problem. Exhibit a Golod group Γ, a subgroup H ≤ Γ, an infinite AT-group A in the sense above, and a surjective group homomorphism φ : H → A. Equivalently: prove that some Golod group has a subgroup that maps homomorphically onto an infinite AT-group, and construct such a triple (Γ, H, φ) explicitly.

